WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.840
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.200
Today we are going to perform pose estimation,
with an amazing twist.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.549
You’ll love it!

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:14.400
But wait, what is pose estimation?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:22.230
Well, simple, a video of people goes in, and
the posture they are taking comes out.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:28.750
Now, you see here that previous techniques
can already do this quite well, even for videos.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:32.700
So, by today, the game has evolved.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:39.430
Just pose estimation is not that new, we need
pose estimation plus something else.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.220
We need a little extra, if you will.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.060
So, what can that little extra be?

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.150
Let’s look at three examples.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:54.490
For instance, one, NVIDIA has an highly advanced
pose estimation technique that can refine

00:00:54.490 --> 00:01:00.840
its estimations by putting these humans into
a virtual physics simulation.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:07.610
Without that, this kind of foot sliding often
happens, but after the physics simulation,

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.950
not anymore.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:14.110
As a result, it can understand even this explosive
sprinting motion.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.770
This dynamic tennis serve.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:17.770
You name it.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.800
All of them are very close.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.060
So, what is this good for?

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:28.450
Well, many things, but here is my favorite
- if we can track the motion well, we can

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:36.659
put it onto a virtual character, so we ourselves
can move around in a beautiful, imagined world.

00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:38.570
So, that was one.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:45.440
Pose estimation + something extra, where the
something extra is a physics simulation.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.440
Nice.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:54.909
Now, two, if we allow an AI to read the wifi-signals
bouncing around in a room, it can perform

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:59.300
pose estimation, even through walls.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.300
Kind of.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.630
Once again, pose estimation with something
extra.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:10.270
And three, this is pose estimation with inertial
sensors.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.739
This works when playing a friendly game of
table tennis with a friend…or…wait.

00:02:14.739 --> 00:02:20.910
Or maybe, a not so friendly game of table
tennis.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.510
And this works really well even in the dark.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:30.500
So, all of these are pose estimation plus
something extra.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:37.770
And now, let’s have a look at this new paper,
which performs pose estimation, plus…well,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.680
pose estimation as it seems.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.020
Okay, I don’t see anything nothing new here,
really.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.760
Why is this work on Two Minute Papers?

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.770
Well, now hold on to your papers, and check
this out.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:52.770
Yes!

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.180
Oh yes!

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.850
So what is this?

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:57.730
Well, here is the twist.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:04.840
We can give a piece of video to this AI, write
a piece of text as you see up here, and it

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:11.180
will not only find what we are looking for
in the video, mark it, and then, even track

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.140
it over time.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.990
Now that is really cool.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:20.270
We just say what we want to be tracked over
the video, and it will do it automatically.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:24.690
It can find the dog and the capybara.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:30.550
These are rather rudimentary descriptions,
but it is by no means limited to that.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:38.560
Look, we can also say a man wearing a white
shirt and blue shorts riding a surfboard.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.019
And, yes!

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:41.540
It can find it.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:48.440
And, also we can add a description of the
surfboard, and it can tell which is which.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.150
And I like the tracking too.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:57.140
This scene has tons of high-frequency changes,
lots of occlusion, and it is still doing really

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.240
well.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.240
Loving it.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:05.120
So, I am thinking that this helps us take
full advantage of the text descriptions.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.150
Look.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:12.960
We can ask it to mark the parrot and the cockatoo,
and it knows which is which.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.400
So I can imagine more advanced applications
where we need to find the appropriate kind

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.430
of animal or object among many others, and
we don’t even need to know what to look

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.430
for.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.939
Just say what you want, and it will find it!

00:04:27.939 --> 00:04:33.689
I also liked how this is done with a transformer
neural network that can jointly process the

00:04:33.689 --> 00:04:37.800
text and video in one elegant solution.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.339
That is really cool.

00:04:39.339 --> 00:04:46.020
Now, of course, every single one of you Fellow
Scholars can see that this is not perfect.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.080
Not even close.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.800
Depending on the input, temporal coherence
issues can arise, these are the jumpy artifacts

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.490
from frame to frame.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:59.389
But, still, this is swift progress in machine
learning research.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:06.639
We could only do this in 2018 and were very
happy about it, and just a couple papers down

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:11.150
the line, and now, we just say what we want
and the AI will do it.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.830
And just imagine what we will have a couple
more papers down the line.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.580
I cannot wait.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.419
So, what would you use this for?

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:22.830
Please let me know in the comments below!

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:28.949
And wait, the source code, an interactive
demo, and a notebook are available in the

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:30.259
video description.

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:32.419
So, you know what to do.

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:53.180
Yes, let the experiments begin!

00:05:53.180 --> 00:06:28.659
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

